  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front will drop in from the northwest today, which will \nbring rain showers to the area. Instability will be lacking, so\nthunderstorms are not expected at this time from the front. \nTemperatures today will vary depending on location. For most of\nCentral NY north of Interstate 88, the earlier cold front \npassage will likely keep highs in the 60s. However, farther \nsouth (Northeast PA to the Catskills), the later cold front \npassage will likely result in highs mainly in the 70s, and \nperhaps near 80 degrees in the Wyoming Valley.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure will bring fantastic fall-weather to the area on\nSaturday with seasonably cool temperatures (highs mainly in the\n60s) and plenty of sunshine.\n\nOn Sunday, our region will be sandwiched between a low pressure\nsystem passing by well to the south and a trough dipping down\nfrom Canada. The most likely outcome is mainly dry conditions\nfor the area, but a few rain showers may sneak into Northeast\nPA from the southern branch system. Otherwise, Sunday will be \npartly to mostly sunny with highs in the 60s once again.\n\nMuch cooler weather looks to move into the region early next\nweek as the influence from a deep longwave trough dipping\nsouthward from Canada becomes more apparent. With a surface high\npressure system likely in place, the first widespread \nfrost/freeze is possible if skies can remain clear Monday night \nand especially Tuesday night. That being said, the growing\nseason has ended for most of the area (except some of the \nFinger Lakes counties in NY and all of Northeast PA). So any\npotential Frost Advisories or Freeze Watches/Warnings would only\nbe issued for the remaining active areas.\n\nThe high pressure is also expected to bring a prolonged period \nof dry weather through most of next week.",
